{
  "id": "Compound::DB01628 - Gene::1576",
  "verbalization": "Etoricoxib, a nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drug, is shown to bind to Cyp2e1 and Cyp2c19, both of which are involved in oxidative metabolism. These enzymes are known to participate in monooxygenase and oxidoreductase activities, which are critical for the metabolism of various substrates, including xenobiotics. The evidence suggests that Etoricoxib may influence Cyp3a4 through its interaction with these enzymes, as Cyp3a4 is a known target of these metabolic activities. The integration of these pathways highlights a potential mechanism by which Etoricoxib could modulate Cyp3a4 function, likely through indirect interactions that involve the metabolic pathways of Cyp2e1 and Cyp2c19. This relationship underscores the role of these enzymes in the metabolic clearance of Etoricoxib and suggests a broader impact on drug metabolism.",
